WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:12.024
This is Chapter Two, Episode Four: general elections, the voting system, how a law is made, and direct democracy.

3
00:00:12.574 --> 00:00:16.386
Free elections are among the most important things in a democracy.

4
00:00:16.336 --> 00:00:24.474
At a general election, the individual voter has influence on which politicians and which political parties are to sit in the Folketing.

5
00:00:24.474 --> 00:00:30.824
Here the voters can express their views on the policy so far, and on the policy they want in the future.

6
00:00:30.824 --> 00:00:35.049
The individual voter has no duty to vote at a general election.

7
00:00:35.049 --> 00:00:45.049
Since the year 2000, between 84 and 88 percent of voters, on average 86 percent, have voted when there has been a general election.

8
00:00:45.049 --> 00:00:49.986
That is a high share compared with most other countries, also Denmark's neighbours.

9
00:00:49.986 --> 00:01:08.082
In Sweden, voter turnout has, by comparison, been 84 percent, in Norway 78 percent, in Germany 76 percent, and in the United Kingdom 65 percent, when there have been elections to those countries' national parliaments since 2000.

10
00:01:08.632 --> 00:01:17.364
In Danish, a general election is a folketingsvalg, a municipal election is a kommunalvalg, and voter turnout is valgdeltagelse.

11
00:01:17.914 --> 00:01:21.889
A general election must be held at least every four years.

12
00:01:21.839 --> 00:01:23.664
This is in the constitution.

13
00:01:23.664 --> 00:01:33.689
The members of the Folketing are thus elected for at most four years at a time, but there is no upper limit to how many times a member can stand for the Folketing and be elected.

14
00:01:33.689 --> 00:01:39.814
It is the prime minister who is responsible for calling a new election before the election period runs out.

15
00:01:39.814 --> 00:01:44.814
That can be after the four years, or earlier, if the prime minister wishes.

16
00:01:45.364 --> 00:01:50.189
General elections are held as general, direct and secret elections.

17
00:01:50.139 --> 00:01:54.802
General means that all persons with Danish citizenship have the right to vote.

18
00:01:54.802 --> 00:02:05.502
They must, however, be eighteen years old, the age of majority, and have a permanent residence in the realm, that is, in Denmark, in Greenland or in the Faroe Islands.

19
00:02:05.502 --> 00:02:10.002
Direct means that you vote directly for the candidates or parties that are standing.

20
00:02:10.002 --> 00:02:15.864
Secret means that you as a voter can freely cast your vote without others being able to see who you vote for.

21
00:02:16.414 --> 00:02:22.701
A person who has the right to vote for the Folketing normally also meets the conditions to be elected to the Folketing.

22
00:02:22.651 --> 00:02:25.239
This is called being eligible, valgbar.

23
00:02:25.239 --> 00:02:33.339
To be eligible, you must among other things have Danish citizenship, be eighteen years old and have a permanent residence in the realm.

24
00:02:33.339 --> 00:02:39.939
But you must not have been punished for something that, in people's eyes, makes you unworthy of sitting in the Folketing.

25
00:02:39.939 --> 00:02:45.042
It is the Folketing that decides whether a convicted member should be regarded as unworthy.

26
00:02:45.592 --> 00:02:47.417
Now the voting system.

27
00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:51.454
If you wish to stand at a general election, you can do it in two ways.

28
00:02:51.454 --> 00:03:00.267
You can stand as a candidate for a party that has the right to take part in the election, a so-called party eligible to stand, which then has to approve you.

29
00:03:00.267 --> 00:03:02.529
This is by far the most common.

30
00:03:02.529 --> 00:03:08.355
All parties that were elected at the last general election are automatically eligible to stand.

31
00:03:08.355 --> 00:03:11.179
For other parties, special rules apply.

32
00:03:11.179 --> 00:03:20.342
For a party to become eligible to stand, it must have collected a certain number of signatures, which must be approved by the Minister of the Interior.

33
00:03:20.342 --> 00:03:23.542
Normally this means about 20,000 signatures.

34
00:03:23.542 --> 00:03:28.654
But you can also stand as a candidate and be elected to the Folketing outside the parties.

35
00:03:28.654 --> 00:03:35.579
Then at least 150 voters in your constituency must sign to say they recommend you, before you can stand.

36
00:03:35.579 --> 00:03:37.908
Very few have been elected in this way.

37
00:03:38.458 --> 00:03:44.420
All eligible voters living in Denmark automatically receive a polling card, a valgkort.

38
00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:48.270
On election day, the citizen can vote in two different ways.

39
00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:51.433
You can vote for a specific politician, a candidate.

40
00:03:51.433 --> 00:03:53.370
This is called a personal vote.

41
00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.745
You can also vote for a political party.

42
00:03:55.745 --> 00:03:57.470
This is called a party vote.

43
00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:00.083
You may only put one cross on the ballot paper.

44
00:04:00.083 --> 00:04:03.845
So you cannot vote for several parties or several candidates.

45
00:04:03.845 --> 00:04:10.566
When the votes have been counted, the seats are distributed in the Folketing according to a method decided in advance.

46
00:04:11.116 --> 00:04:17.653
When the general election is over, the elected parties and members negotiate about who should form a government.

47
00:04:17.603 --> 00:04:25.428
All voters can also vote by post in advance of the election, instead of turning up at the polling station and voting on election day itself.

48
00:04:25.428 --> 00:04:33.391
This often happens by showing up at Citizen Service, Borgerservice, in your municipality and casting your vote in an envelope.

49
00:04:33.391 --> 00:04:45.024
But postal votes can also often be cast, for example, in hospitals and nursing homes, where many voters have difficulty getting to the polling stations because of illness or old age.

50
00:04:45.574 --> 00:04:54.461
The Danish election system is arranged so that the composition of the Folketing, as far as possible, reflects who the voters have voted for.

51
00:04:54.411 --> 00:04:58.961
The seats are distributed by the proportional method, forholdstalsmetoden.

52
00:04:58.961 --> 00:05:07.049
That means a political party as far as possible gets the same share of the seats in the Folketing as the party's share of the valid votes cast.

53
00:05:07.049 --> 00:05:15.546
If a party has, for example, received ten percent of the votes, it should, roughly speaking, have ten percent of the seats.

54
00:05:16.096 --> 00:05:23.758
The Danish election system normally requires a party to get at least two percent of the valid votes cast to get into the Folketing.

55
00:05:23.708 --> 00:05:30.058
This so-called electoral threshold, the spærregrænse, is lower than in many neighbouring countries.

56
00:05:30.058 --> 00:05:36.558
In Sweden and Norway it is, for example, four percent, and in Germany five percent.

57
00:05:36.558 --> 00:05:45.946
In practice this means that more parties through the years have been represented in the Folketing in comparison with the parliaments of the neighbouring countries mentioned.

58
00:05:45.946 --> 00:05:52.733
Since 1973, between seven and twelve parties have been elected to the Folketing at each election.

59
00:05:52.733 --> 00:06:02.696
In the Netherlands, for example, there is no electoral threshold, so a party is represented in parliament if it just gets enough votes for a single seat nationwide.

60
00:06:02.696 --> 00:06:07.524
The Dutch parliament has therefore typically had even more parties than the Danish one.

61
00:06:08.074 --> 00:06:10.674
Now it is time for your exam practice.

62
00:06:10.624 --> 00:06:15.536
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

63
00:06:15.536 --> 00:06:23.022
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

64
00:06:23.572 --> 00:06:24.822
Question 1.

65
00:06:24.772 --> 00:06:28.547
What share of voters normally vote at elections to the Folketing?

66
00:06:28.547 --> 00:06:31.684
Option A: 60 to 69 percent.

67
00:06:31.684 --> 00:06:34.984
Option B: 70 to 79 percent.

68
00:06:34.984 --> 00:06:38.112
Option C: 80 to 89 percent.
[DA] Hvor stor en andel af vælgerne stemmer normalt ved valg til Folketinget?  A: 60-69 %  |  B: 70-79 %  |  C: 80-89 %

69
00:06:44.662 --> 00:06:47.874
The answer is C: 80 to 89 percent.

70
00:06:47.824 --> 00:06:52.170
It appeared in the June 2020 and November 2025 exams.
[DA] ✔ C: 80-89 %

71
00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:53.970
Question 2.

72
00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:59.457
How many percent of the votes at a general election must a party normally get to enter the Folketing?

73
00:06:59.457 --> 00:07:01.945
Option A: At least 2 percent.

74
00:07:01.945 --> 00:07:04.632
Option B: At least 4 percent.

75
00:07:04.632 --> 00:07:07.452
Option C: At least 6 percent.
[DA] Hvor mange procent af stemmerne ved et folketingsvalg skal et parti normalt have for at komme i Folketinget?  A: Mindst 2 %  |  B: Mindst 4 %  |  C: Mindst 6 %

76
00:07:14.002 --> 00:07:16.564
The answer is A: At least 2 percent.

77
00:07:16.514 --> 00:07:20.886
It appeared in the June 2021 and November 2024 exams.
[DA] ✔ A: Mindst 2 %

78
00:07:21.436 --> 00:07:22.773
Question 3.

79
00:07:22.723 --> 00:07:26.773
How often must there be a general election according to the constitution?

80
00:07:26.773 --> 00:07:29.611
Option A: At least every third year.

81
00:07:29.611 --> 00:07:32.473
Option B: At least every fourth year.

82
00:07:32.473 --> 00:07:35.232
Option C: At least every fifth year.
[DA] Hvor ofte skal der ifølge grundloven være folketingsvalg i Danmark?  A: Mindst hvert tredje år  |  B: Mindst hvert fjerde år  |  C: Mindst hvert femte år

83
00:07:41.782 --> 00:07:45.019
The answer is B: At least every fourth year.

84
00:07:44.969 --> 00:07:47.874
It appeared in the November 2021 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: Mindst hvert fjerde år

85
00:07:48.424 --> 00:07:50.824
That's the end of Part One of Episode 4.

86
00:07:50.774 --> 00:07:55.911
In Part Two, we continue with how a law is made, and direct democracy.

87
00:07:55.911 --> 00:07:56.964
See you there.

